00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.330
- When preparing for
consulting interviews,

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:05.200
people focus so much on the case study

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.300
part of the interview,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.520
but other parts such as
the experience interviews

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.520
are often overlooked by candidates

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.967
and are not properly prepared,

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:14.060
and this can really be a deal breaker

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:15.570
for your consulting application.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:16.960
Today, we are going to talk about

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.350
how you pass interviews at
management consulting firms,

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.700
so it doesn't matter if you want to apply

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.077
to an MBB firm or a big
four consulting firm.

00:00:24.077 --> 00:00:26.190
This video has you covered.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.200
Hi guys, my name is
Heinrich Rusche and come

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.663
to another coffee break here
on my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:33.619
On my channel, I want to
help you to become successful

00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:36.290
in the first years of your career,

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.460
so it doesn't matter if
you're working in consulting

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.730
and investment banking or
in a corporate context.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:41.880
I am here to help you.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.660
As always, if you're taking any value

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:44.770
out of this video at all,

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.410
please hit the like button
for the YouTube algorithm

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.840
and also subscribe to this
channel for weekly videos of me.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.453
And if you want to see even more content,

00:00:52.453 --> 00:00:53.760
I also have an Instagram.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.450
My handle is @firmlearning,

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.050
so follow me on my Instagram as well

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.760
to see even more content from me.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.110
Since I made this channel,

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:01.440
many of you have approached me

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.860
and asked me to talk about
the interview process

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.140
of management consulting firms.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.370
What does it take to
pass these interviews?

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.535
How exactly are these
interviews structured,

00:01:10.535 --> 00:01:13.225
and what are my tips and tricks

00:01:13.225 --> 00:01:16.050
to help you get the job that you want.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.190
And I'm really passionate about this topic

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.520
because the great thing
for you as a candidate

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.738
is that it doesn't matter what
consulting firm you apply to,

00:01:22.738 --> 00:01:26.430
the key building blocks of the
interview that you will face

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.140
are pretty similar among
all of the firms out there.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.950
So and of course there are
differences among these firms.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.849
If you understand these
basic building blocks

00:01:33.849 --> 00:01:36.095
that consulting interviews are made of,

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:38.210
then you will be really well set

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:39.700
for all of these interviews to come.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:40.557
And the truth is in the end,

00:01:40.557 --> 00:01:42.970
there are three of these building blocks.

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:44.160
Three building blocks

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.702
that most of these consulting
interviews consist of.

00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:50.090
The first is the CV review part,

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.170
so an interview section

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.434
where the interviewer
talks to you about your CV.

00:01:53.434 --> 00:01:56.421
The second is the experience interview

00:01:56.421 --> 00:01:58.801
where the interviewer deep dives with you

00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:00.640
into specific situations,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.943
specific experiences
that you had in the past,

00:02:03.943 --> 00:02:06.270
and the third is the case study interview.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.890
This is the section where the interviewer

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.969
discusses with you a real business problem

00:02:09.969 --> 00:02:12.292
and wants to understand
how you would handle

00:02:12.292 --> 00:02:14.930
these kinds of problems as a candidate,

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:16.840
because of course this would be the task

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.680
that you are facing as a consultant.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.793
Beyond then, there are some
other interview types as well

00:02:20.793 --> 00:02:23.486
that some of the firms use,

00:02:23.486 --> 00:02:25.330
but these more exotic
components are not the focus

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:28.410
of this video because today I
really want to drill it down

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:31.590
to the basics to help you
understand what you can expect

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:32.423
in these interviews.

00:02:32.423 --> 00:02:34.609
So this video is structured
now in three parts.

00:02:34.609 --> 00:02:38.070
First I'm going to talk about
the CV review interview,

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.280
second about the experience interview,

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.640
and third about the case study interview.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.693
And guys, if you get these
three types interviews right,

00:02:44.693 --> 00:02:47.970
then you will get that consulting job.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.450
But before now we jump
into these three sections,

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.360
I have prepared something
really special for you.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.342
I prepared a consulting
interview preparation checklist

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:59.110
that is a list of to dos that I believe

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.190
that you should do to prepare
yourself for these interviews.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.510
It is structured along these
different types of interviews

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.320
and it also gives some tips

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.760
on these other more
exotic interview formats,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.820
and from my perspective,

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.593
if you do exactly what I'm
writing in this checklist,

00:03:12.593 --> 00:03:14.300
then the chances are pretty high

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.810
that you are well prepared
for the interviews to come.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.010
So if you are interested
to get this checklist

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.790
that is completely free for you,

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:22.550
just look into the
video description below,

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:25.032
if there you'll subscribe to my newsletter

00:03:25.032 --> 00:03:26.680
you will receive the
checklist as a download link,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.730
and if you are already
subscribed to the newsletter,

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:29.940
of course, no problem,

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:30.927
just enter you email again

00:03:30.927 --> 00:03:33.630
and you will get that checklist as well.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.310
Let's start with section number one,

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.840
and this is the CV review interview,

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.420
and as with all the other
interview types to come,

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.500
this can either be an
independent interview session

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.137
with an interviewer only covering your CV,

00:03:45.137 --> 00:03:47.450
but it can also be integrated in a session

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.620
where you both have a CV review section

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.120
and then later an experience interview

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.577
or later a case study or a
combination of all of the three,

00:03:53.577 --> 00:03:55.330
so it's not necessarily the case

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:58.050
that you have a full interview
only discussing your CV,

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.500
but often it is also
integrated with other parts.

00:04:01.416 --> 00:04:02.770
So what is the objective
of the CV review interview?

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.210
Why are consulting firms

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.750
doing this type of interview at all?

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:08.240
And the truth is, usually
the consulting firms

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.810
do not use this interview
section to sort out candidates

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.180
based on their CVs,

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:16.360
because this what already
happened in the pre-selection,

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.010
based on your written documents.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.110
So the fact that you got
invited to the interview,

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:21.790
the fact that you are sitting in a room

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:24.200
with an interviewer of the
firm having this interview,

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.223
usually already means that
your CV was good enough.

00:04:27.223 --> 00:04:29.460
The HR department considered your CVs

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.800
to meet all of the criteria to
get invited to the interview,

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.440
because otherwise you wouldn't be here.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.571
So don't go into the
interview with the mindset

00:04:36.571 --> 00:04:38.840
that now you need to prove yourself,

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:40.360
with the mindset that maybe

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.470
you are not good enough on the paper,

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:43.680
because usually you are.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.160
But how do these firms use
this type of interview,

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.912
and it's usually used for two things.

00:04:47.912 --> 00:04:49.890
First, it's just to build rapport,

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.420
and just to get to know you a bit better

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:52.400
on a personal level.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:53.568
Often, the CV review interview

00:04:53.568 --> 00:04:55.350
is rather done in the beginning,

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.250
and one of the earlier,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.800
maybe even the first interview.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.610
It is supposed to break the ice a bit

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.210
because of course as a candidate,

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.567
it is believed that you can
easily talk about yourself

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:06.080
and this puts you in less
stress than for instance,

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.720
doing a more complicated case study.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.040
So just ease up a bit.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.128
Be ready to build some
rapport with your interviewer.

00:05:11.128 --> 00:05:14.100
Get to know each other and
just try to have a good time.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:17.014
However, how some firms do
use the CV review section

00:05:17.014 --> 00:05:18.950
is to drill down a bit,

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.230
to deep dive a bit on some of the things

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:22.440
that you write in your CV,

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.120
and they might especially do
that if they have some reason

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.607
to maybe doubt whether
some things that you write

00:05:27.607 --> 00:05:29.100
are really accurate or not.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.240
So be ready to receive
some critical questions,

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.912
maybe to explain a bit what you did

00:05:32.912 --> 00:05:35.892
on certain elements on the CV
that you are writing about.

00:05:35.892 --> 00:05:38.414
Be ready to go into some level of detail

00:05:38.414 --> 00:05:40.670
to really give the interviewer the comfort

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.190
that of course everything that you wrote

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.320
is completely accurate
and completely correct.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.201
One specific thing that
I want to encourage you

00:05:46.201 --> 00:05:48.812
to prepare in advance for
this interview section

00:05:48.812 --> 00:05:51.813
is to answer a question such as,

00:05:51.813 --> 00:05:54.430
"Please walk me through your CV,"

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:56.810
or, "Please introduce yourself."

00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.450
This is a question commonly asked

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:00.030
by the consultant in the beginning.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.179
This should be a sign for
you to now give a short talk,

00:06:02.179 --> 00:06:05.450
maybe around five minutes, about your CV,

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:06.910
about what you did in the past.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:09.128
So this is something you can
really well prepare in advance

00:06:09.128 --> 00:06:10.920
before the interview,

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.440
so just practice giving a
short, five minute presentation

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:16.070
about your background.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:17.890
Go over the key stations in your life.

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:18.970
Both from a professional,

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:20.710
but also from an educational perspective.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:24.720
What's important here is to not
get lost in the minor stuff.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.292
Do not talk for three
minutes about an internship

00:06:27.292 --> 00:06:29.890
that you did at high school,
or something like that.

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:31.990
Really focus on the things that matter.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.375
Focus on the recent things
that are really important

00:06:34.375 --> 00:06:37.500
and this already is an important
skill in business as well,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:38.790
and especially as in consulting.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.319
The interviewer wants to trust you

00:06:40.319 --> 00:06:42.139
that if you sit in front of a client

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:44.062
and you're having such a
professional conversation,

00:06:44.062 --> 00:06:46.260
that you can distinguish
the things that matter

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.290
from the things that do not matter.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:50.114
So take this already
as a first little test

00:06:50.114 --> 00:06:51.817
to make sure that you are well prepared,

00:06:51.817 --> 00:06:54.390
because this is a question
that very likely will come

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.170
and this is something
that you can very easily

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:57.060
prepare at home.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.730
The second interview section
that you should feel ready for

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:01.540
is the experience interview.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:02.990
In the experience interview,

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.320
the interviewer will go into the details

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:06.910
of specific instances,

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:09.599
specific situations that
you've faced in the past

00:07:09.599 --> 00:07:13.060
and how you handled, how
you solved these instances.

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:14.770
So what exactly are the
interviewers looking for here?

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:15.970
And well of course,

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:18.200
the criteria differ a
bit from firm by firm.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.790
The criteria for the
McKinsey experience interview

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.930
are transparently
written on their website.

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:24.630
They're looking for personal impact,

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:27.460
for entrepreneurial drive,
for problem solving skills,

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:28.870
and for leadership.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:30.100
So let's break that down.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:30.933
What does it mean?

00:07:30.933 --> 00:07:32.100
What are these people looking for?

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:33.710
So let's start with personal impact.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.670
This is really about your achievements.

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:38.630
About the things that you
changed to the better.

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:40.140
Stories about personal impact

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.004
can both be from a business perspective

00:07:42.004 --> 00:07:44.910
or from a social, community perspective,

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:48.380
where you created something
great for your community.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.070
Entrepreneurial drive
can mean many things,

00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:53.300
but to sum it up, for me in a nutshell

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:56.217
it means to leave a bit the normal path.

00:07:56.217 --> 00:07:59.255
To not only do the things
that are expected from you,

00:07:59.255 --> 00:08:02.854
but look for some creative
ways to solve problems,

00:08:02.854 --> 00:08:05.760
to things that other
people have no done before.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.240
Think of it out of the box.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:08.670
This is what you should talk about

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.339
when you address this
entrepreneurship dimension.

00:08:10.339 --> 00:08:14.220
Problem solving skills is about
your analytical abilities.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.160
It is about cutting through complexity

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.460
of complex business challenges.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:20.200
You can also talk about how you solved

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.180
a complex interpersonal
challenge that you had,

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:25.970
maybe also with some other
students in a university course

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.419
that you took, when you really
to make something happen.

00:08:28.419 --> 00:08:31.650
Talk about how you can solve problems

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.157
that other people cannot solve.

00:08:33.157 --> 00:08:35.483
And the last dimension is
your leadership ability.

00:08:35.483 --> 00:08:37.536
While I agree that this
is a bit overlapping

00:08:37.536 --> 00:08:39.670
with some of the other dimensions,

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:41.277
for me, leadership in this context

00:08:41.277 --> 00:08:44.010
mainly means doing away with other people,

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:45.555
so maybe your own experience hire,

00:08:45.555 --> 00:08:47.972
and already had some leadership
experience in the past

00:08:47.972 --> 00:08:49.866
with extra teammates in the job,

00:08:49.866 --> 00:08:52.130
or if you are fresh out of university,

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.681
maybe in university you were
a leading member of a club,

00:08:54.681 --> 00:08:57.660
or maybe of a social organization
that you are a member

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:58.493
of the board of.

00:08:58.493 --> 00:08:59.658
So here, talk about how you were able

00:08:59.658 --> 00:09:01.720
to motivate other people,

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.410
to create a team and as a team
effort to get things done.

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:07.190
This, from my perspective, is leadership

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:09.800
and this is what will be
valued by consulting firms.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:11.940
And again, these are
the McKinsey dimensions.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:13.824
Other consulting firms

00:09:13.824 --> 00:09:14.737
might have slightly different dimensions,

00:09:14.737 --> 00:09:16.020
but in the end of the day,

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.610
these are the things that pretty much

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.270
all of the consulting
firms are looking for,

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:20.904
and if you address in
your experience interview

00:09:20.904 --> 00:09:23.980
these types of dimensions,
you will be perfectly fine.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.200
So what are typical
questions that will be asked

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:27.033
in these interviews?

00:09:27.033 --> 00:09:29.343
Ang again, here they might
me difference firm by firm,

00:09:29.343 --> 00:09:32.235
but usually the questions
are quite predictable.

00:09:32.235 --> 00:09:34.754
You will be asked to talk
about an instance of your past

00:09:34.754 --> 00:09:37.925
that you achieved something
that you are really proud of,

00:09:37.925 --> 00:09:40.256
or you will be asked to
talk about a team conflict

00:09:40.256 --> 00:09:42.575
that you had with another
person in the past,

00:09:42.575 --> 00:09:44.889
and how you were able
to overcome the conflict

00:09:44.889 --> 00:09:47.170
and what you actually did to solve it.

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:49.590
You might be asked about
any specific element

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:51.578
that you have on your
CV to provide a story

00:09:51.578 --> 00:09:54.200
on how you really achieved an impact.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:56.913
How you really made a
difference to the firm,

00:09:56.913 --> 00:09:59.302
to the organization that
you were a member of

00:09:59.302 --> 00:10:01.930
or that you were working for in your CV.

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:03.530
So these are the questions
that you should expect

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:06.230
and hear the first thing
for the preparation

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:08.910
is that these questions
should not surprise you

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:09.743
in the interview.

00:10:09.743 --> 00:10:11.620
You should never put
yourself in a position

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:13.312
that in the interview it's the first time

00:10:13.312 --> 00:10:15.170
that you are thinking about these things,

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:17.180
and on the spot, you need
to come up with a story

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.870
that happened in the past

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:20.820
that addresses the question
that you are asked.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:22.991
If this happens, that you were
clearly not well prepared,

00:10:22.991 --> 00:10:26.590
the likelihood is pretty high
that you will not be able

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:28.803
to just on the spot come up
with the best story to tell

00:10:28.803 --> 00:10:30.330
in these instances,

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.500
therefore, what you absolutely need to do

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.060
as a preparation to these interviews,

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.435
that you at home, you think
about what stories could be

00:10:36.435 --> 00:10:39.870
that would really address
these different elements

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:40.710
that we talked about.

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:43.861
Come up with at least five
stories that you could tell

00:10:43.861 --> 00:10:45.910
to answer questions like that,

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:47.160
and prepare these stories.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.760
Come up with a basic structure

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.260
about how you want to go
about telling these stories.

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:53.063
Think ahead what likely questions will be

00:10:53.063 --> 00:10:56.210
that the interviewer will then
ask you about these stories,

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:57.320
because of course, again,

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:58.790
the interviewer will want to make sure

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:00.510
that you're just not making something up,

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.993
so he might ask some
very detailed questions

00:11:02.993 --> 00:11:04.980
just to fact check you and to make sure

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.210
that what you are talking
about actually really happened.

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:08.420
So be well prepared.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:10.320
Have these stories ready in your head,

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:11.845
so when an interviewer
asks you these questions

00:11:11.845 --> 00:11:13.680
in an experience interview,

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.723
you already know what you
are going to talk about.

00:11:15.723 --> 00:11:17.710
And we got in the experience interview

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:18.790
what I want to end with,

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:20.600
is that many, many people underestimate

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:23.197
is how important this
experience interview is.

00:11:23.197 --> 00:11:25.080
For most consulting firms,

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:26.719
at least based on the
information that I have,

00:11:26.719 --> 00:11:29.344
the experience interview
is easily as important

00:11:29.344 --> 00:11:30.900
as the case studies,

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:32.510
but people in their preparation time

00:11:32.510 --> 00:11:35.120
usually put the vast majority
into the case studies,

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:38.060
but only spend very
little time making sure

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.970
that they get the
experience interview right.

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:41.230
Don't be one of these people.

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:43.030
Make sure that you have your stories

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:44.610
ready and well prepared.

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:46.490
This way you will make a great impression

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:47.910
in your experience interviews

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:50.340
and then the experience interview
will be a success for you.

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:52.290
Now to the third section of this video,

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:54.360
and this is the case study interview.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:56.452
The case study interview
is the most iconic part

00:11:56.452 --> 00:11:59.300
of interviewing with consulting firms.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:00.443
This is the type of interview

00:12:00.443 --> 00:12:03.090
that is most specific to consulting.

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:04.760
Usually if you're
interviewing in a corporate

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:06.430
or even in a banking situation,

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:07.960
you do not have case studies,

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:10.330
at least in the number and
in the depth that you have

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:11.640
in a consulting interview.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.542
Now for sure, I could
go into lots of detail

00:12:13.542 --> 00:12:15.450
on the case study interview.

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:17.251
This alone would be material
for a whole new video.

00:12:17.251 --> 00:12:20.750
Probably it would be material
for a multi hour course

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:22.988
to get ready for this
case study interview.

00:12:22.988 --> 00:12:25.680
But in this video, I just want
to give you the highlights.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.380
I just want to give you
the basic structure,

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:29.600
the basic things that you
need to have in your mind

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:30.920
when facing such an interview.

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:32.150
So what can you expect

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:33.577
when going into a case study interview?

00:12:33.577 --> 00:12:36.549
The interviewer will often
pitch you a business problem

00:12:36.549 --> 00:12:39.057
and talk about a challenging
business situation

00:12:39.057 --> 00:12:41.244
that now you are supposed to solve,

00:12:41.244 --> 00:12:43.960
that you are not supposed
to give your thinking,

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:45.030
your structuring on.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.210
These business problems are often adapted

00:12:47.210 --> 00:12:49.435
from actual projects that the interviewer

00:12:49.435 --> 00:12:51.440
was working on in the past.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:53.370
The purpose of all of this is of course

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:54.862
to now discuss with you issues

00:12:54.862 --> 00:12:56.881
that you might very well face,

00:12:56.881 --> 00:12:59.140
that you might very well experience

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:01.360
in your work as a consultant as well.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:02.193
And of course,

00:13:02.193 --> 00:13:03.470
now the firms are interested to see

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:05.440
whether you can handle these problems.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.800
Whether you can come up
with some valuable insight,

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:10.710
some valuable structures that
would be actually helpful

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:12.393
in a real consulting project as well.

00:13:12.393 --> 00:13:14.640
And one of the most important things

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:16.130
when you now face such a case study

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:17.220
is that you need to understand

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.680
what type of case study you are facing,

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:21.420
and hear that there are
probably at least two

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.090
important distinctions that
you need to understand.

00:13:24.090 --> 00:13:26.073
The first is the whole
format of the case study,

00:13:26.073 --> 00:13:28.400
and generally you can distinguish

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.552
two types of case study formats.

00:13:30.552 --> 00:13:33.605
There's the interviewer-led
case study format

00:13:33.605 --> 00:13:37.374
and the interviewee-led case study format.

00:13:37.374 --> 00:13:39.783
So how do these two formats
distinguish themselves?

00:13:39.783 --> 00:13:41.587
In the interviewer-led format,

00:13:41.587 --> 00:13:44.688
the interviewer will lead
the whole conversation.

00:13:44.688 --> 00:13:47.160
He will guide you through the case study

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:48.914
and he will go question by question.

00:13:48.914 --> 00:13:50.862
He will present a little part

00:13:50.862 --> 00:13:52.913
of such a more complex business problem,

00:13:52.913 --> 00:13:55.500
and he will expect you to
now come up with an answer,

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:57.058
to come up with a solution to this part.

00:13:57.058 --> 00:13:59.060
And once you have a bit of a discussion

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:00.550
about this specific part,

00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:02.120
he might completely change the topic

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:04.050
to another aspect of this old case study,

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:05.440
and then he will go into the details

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:07.130
about this new part with you.

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:07.963
So this is now a format

00:14:07.963 --> 00:14:09.964
where you are much more
on the receiving end.

00:14:09.964 --> 00:14:12.380
You are not supposed to
now guide the interviewer

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:13.870
through his case study,

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.552
but it's rather you answering questions

00:14:15.552 --> 00:14:17.449
that you will get on a
specific business problem.

00:14:17.449 --> 00:14:20.311
This interviewer-led format
is famously used by McKinsey

00:14:20.311 --> 00:14:23.300
as their go-to format in their interviews,

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.270
but of course many other consulting firms

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:27.809
use the interviewer-led format as well.

00:14:27.809 --> 00:14:29.680
The interviewee-led
format is a bit different

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:32.064
in the sense that now
you as the interviewee,

00:14:32.064 --> 00:14:33.760
now you as the candidate,

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.800
you are now into the driving
seat of this whole interview.

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:39.230
And what this means is that
usually at the beginning

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.220
the interviewer will give
you a bit of context,

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.210
a bit of a situation or they describe

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:44.636
the overall problem for you.

00:14:44.636 --> 00:14:46.930
So for instance, he might
talk about a business.

00:14:46.930 --> 00:14:48.840
Give you some specifics on that business,

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:50.469
and now will ask a rather open question

00:14:50.469 --> 00:14:54.430
of what this business should
do to improve its profits.

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:56.523
But now you are in the
lead as a candidate.

00:14:56.523 --> 00:14:58.740
You are in the lead as an interviewee.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:02.001
Now the interviewer will
expect you to now ask questions

00:15:02.001 --> 00:15:04.990
to the interviewer about
additional information

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:06.250
that you now want to have

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:07.960
in order now to come to a solution.

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:09.667
So here, now do not wait
that the interviewer

00:15:09.667 --> 00:15:11.566
gives all of the relevant
information to you,

00:15:11.566 --> 00:15:14.320
but you are now supposed to really ask,

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:16.210
to ask the interviewer all of the things

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:17.510
that you believe are relevant.

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:19.100
All of the things that you want to know

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:20.280
to now come to a conclusion.

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:21.590
The second key distinction

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:23.170
between different types of case studies

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:25.440
or maybe also between different question

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:26.660
within one case study

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.681
is whether this is rather
qualitative or quantitative answer

00:15:29.681 --> 00:15:31.780
that you are now supposed to give.

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.220
So questions looking
for qualitative answers

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:35.687
are for instance, something like,

00:15:35.687 --> 00:15:37.465
"Okay, please tell me
about the different aspects

00:15:37.465 --> 00:15:39.550
"that are driving this problem," or,

00:15:39.550 --> 00:15:41.517
"Tell me about the different ways,

00:15:41.517 --> 00:15:43.920
"what a company can do to cut its costs."

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.680
These are questions that are
looking for qualitative answers

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.340
and with these qualitative questions,

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:50.490
what you are supposed
to do is not to just say

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.260
the first things that
come through your head,

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:54.054
but it's rather to come
up with a structure,

00:15:54.054 --> 00:15:57.700
to propose this structure to
your interviewer as a solution.

00:15:57.700 --> 00:15:59.760
These qualitative case study questions

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:01.896
you can look up lots and
lots of frameworks online

00:16:01.896 --> 00:16:04.340
on how you can approach and solve

00:16:04.340 --> 00:16:06.490
these different types of
qualitative questions.

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.300
He wants to see that
you are a structured guy

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.240
who can in a repeatable
way propose solutions

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:12.920
to complex business situations.

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.360
So in contrast to these
qualitative questions,

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:17.143
there are now also the
quantitative questions.

00:16:17.143 --> 00:16:20.053
Here what you are supposed
to do are calculations.

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:22.640
If you are asked to come up
now with a certain number,

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:23.920
with a certain result.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:26.067
Here you are now really
supposed to do some math,

00:16:26.067 --> 00:16:27.613
to do some calculations.

00:16:27.613 --> 00:16:29.452
What you should really do to practice

00:16:29.452 --> 00:16:31.123
for these kind of interview questions

00:16:31.123 --> 00:16:33.983
is to do math calculations both
in your head as mental math,

00:16:33.983 --> 00:16:36.300
but also with pen and paper.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.403
So practice to do complex
divisions or multiplications

00:16:38.403 --> 00:16:40.534
with pen and paper without a calculator,

00:16:40.534 --> 00:16:42.520
because in these interviews

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:44.790
you are usually not given a calculator.

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:47.210
So what can you do to really prepare well

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:48.350
for these case studies?

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:50.700
There's one thing that
I see many people do,

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:53.940
but I just do not believe
that it's really a good thing.

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:56.973
This is going online and
learning twenty, thirty, forty,

00:16:56.973 --> 00:16:59.680
fifty types of different
frameworks, a ton,

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:02.398
just hoping that you will get
asked the exact same question

00:17:02.398 --> 00:17:04.844
that now in this mock case
study online was asked

00:17:04.844 --> 00:17:07.390
and then hoping that by
this you will come up

00:17:07.390 --> 00:17:08.370
with the perfect answer.

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:11.050
This is usually not what
will help you to do well.

00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:12.293
Yes, it's okay and also helpful

00:17:12.293 --> 00:17:14.360
to have a couple of
frameworks in your head,

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:16.229
a couple of frameworks that
you can also easily tailor

00:17:16.229 --> 00:17:18.092
to different types of questions,

00:17:18.092 --> 00:17:20.060
and then this will help you to answer

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:22.573
especially the qualitative
types of interview questions,

00:17:22.573 --> 00:17:25.600
but be reasonable and
don't do too much on that.

00:17:25.600 --> 00:17:28.950
Instead, what you should do
is to do mock case studies

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:31.321
with other actual people.

00:17:31.321 --> 00:17:32.950
What I did when I prepared
for case study interviews

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:33.803
back in the day,

00:17:34.867 --> 00:17:35.700
when I interviewed with McKinsey,

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:37.370
is that I partnered up with another person

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:39.655
that was also interviewing
with MBB consulting firms

00:17:39.655 --> 00:17:42.067
and we met once a week
or so and always prepared

00:17:42.067 --> 00:17:44.856
about two case studies for each other

00:17:44.856 --> 00:17:46.330
every session that we met.

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:47.590
And then we interviewed us.

00:17:47.590 --> 00:17:48.790
We had such an interview,

00:17:48.790 --> 00:17:51.070
and then we gave us
feedback on what went well,

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:52.137
what didn't went well,

00:17:52.137 --> 00:17:55.650
and this for me really was super useful

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:57.560
for getting these case
study interviews right.

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:59.606
So how many case studies
should you do to feel ready?

00:17:59.606 --> 00:18:02.997
Well probably three,
four, five is too little.

00:18:02.997 --> 00:18:07.280
Probably 30, 40, 50 is just far too much.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:08.718
You will just go crazy at some point.

00:18:08.718 --> 00:18:10.457
What I would recommend you is to aim

00:18:10.457 --> 00:18:12.694
for something like 10
to 20 mock case studies.

00:18:12.694 --> 00:18:15.483
This should be completely fine
to get you used to the format

00:18:15.483 --> 00:18:17.650
by doing ten plus mock interviews,

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:19.310
ideally also with different people,

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:20.710
you will build some confidence

00:18:20.710 --> 00:18:22.840
that you can actually
master this interview type.

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.180
And this from my perspective
was the most important part.

00:18:25.180 --> 00:18:26.480
So these are my suggestions

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:28.830
for the three most
important interview parts.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.160
First, the CV review.

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:31.307
Second, the experience interview

00:18:31.307 --> 00:18:33.460
and then the third, the
case study interview.

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:35.034
Now, if you want to learn even more

00:18:35.034 --> 00:18:37.993
about how you can ace
the consulting interview,

00:18:37.993 --> 00:18:40.513
have a look at the checklist
that I have prepared for you

00:18:40.513 --> 00:18:42.640
for the really actionable to dos

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:44.090
that you need to get right

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:45.357
before you now sit in such an interview.

00:18:45.357 --> 00:18:47.680
Again, you can find the download link

